label: Route County
description: >-
  Decide which trust path this parcel's county resolves through. Osceola,
  Manistee and Roscommon go direct to the county's own FeatureServer; Iosco and
  Otsego transit app.fetchgis.com.

  This node does not exist in blue-ribbon. `getCountyAdapter(county)`
  (apps/etl/src/counties/registry.ts) returns a `CountyParcelAdapter` and the
  distinction dies there. Making it a `oneOf` is weir's "a decision becomes a
  type" applied to the finding that motivated this exercise.

  SHARPENED 2026-09-29. #1 and #11 largely dissolved on re-examination (see
  RawParcelFeature.edge), and what they dissolved INTO is this friction, which
  is now the load-bearing one. Restating it with the right weight:

  The honest modelling is five edges with five real schemas. At five counties
  that is fine and arguably better than blue-ribbon's single interface. But
  02_MICHIGAN_GIS_DATA_SOURCES.md wants statewide coverage: MICHIGAN HAS 83
  COUNTIES. 83 edges and 83 nodes whose only differences are
  {fieldName, areaFormula, pinSeparator} is configuration wearing an ontology
  costume. weir cannot say "these N edges are one edge under a renaming",
  which is exactly what a schema mapping is, and generating 83 .edge files
  from a table fights the one-place-to-write-it-down property that makes the
  filename the name.

  ORIGINAL TEXT FOLLOWS.

  FRICTION (#2, the shape of an adapter): weir has no interfaces, typeclasses,
  or dispatch. blue-ribbon has ONE interface with FIVE implementations, which
  is the correct factoring -- each county differs only in URL, field names and
  an area formula. In weir I get two choices:

    (a) five nodes, fetchOsceola..fetchOtsego, and a five-way oneOf. Honest
        about the differences, but every downstream node then takes anyOf of
        five edges and the graph is five parallel copies of one shape.
    (b) two nodes split by TRUST rather than by county, with county as a field.

  I took (b), and I think it is genuinely better than what blue-ribbon has --
  but note what made it work: the five counties differ along an axis (trust)
  that has only two values. If they differed along an axis with five values I
  would have been forced into (a), and weir would have made the code worse.
  weir has no way to say "these five things vary only in configuration".
input: ParcelRequest
output:
  oneOf:
    - DirectCountyQuery
    - ProxiedCountyQuery
examples:
  - given:
      ParcelRequest:
        pin: "10-003-013-20"
        county: "Osceola"
    expect:
      DirectCountyQuery:
        pin: "10-003-013-20"
        county: "Osceola"
        endpoint: "https://services8.arcgis.com/FmKMwUEmDSC75SQm/arcgis/rest/services/OsceolaCountyParcels_view/FeatureServer/0/query"
  - given:
      ParcelRequest:
        pin: "062-026-300-020-00"
        county: "Iosco"
    expect:
      ProxiedCountyQuery:
        pin: "062-026-300-020-00"
        county: "Iosco"
        endpoint: "https://services3.arcgis.com/iosco/FeatureServer/0/query"
        proxyHost: "app.fetchgis.com"
        referer: "https://app.fetchgis.com/?currentMap=iosco"
properties:
  - name: the PIN is never altered by routing
    description: >-
      Routing decides where to ask, never what to ask about. Stated against
      both branches because a router that rewrote the PIN on one path only
      would otherwise pass.
    expr:
      eq:
        - get: output.pin
        - get: input.pin
